## Redundancy: what's now derivable, and what still isn't

A field is redundant only if a **deterministic post-processor** can fill it back in from what a
model emitted, without re-implementing simulator rules. "Shadowed" (same tick+position as a more
specific event) was measured as 100% for kind 22's *locator* fields in a prior pass over this
format; re-affirmed here, not re-measured, since no code affecting that changed since.

### Confirmed redundant (locator only, not the payload) — CORRECTED

**Earlier claim in this file overstated this.** "100% shadowed at the same `(tick, position)`" is
true but was written up as if `(tick, position)` uniquely identifies *which* write a semantic event
explains. It doesn't: measured directly, 20.8–34.5% of `BlockStateChanged` writes share a
`(tick, position)` with at least one other write to the same cell in the same tick (movableRCA
20.8%, tank 34.5%, 1-wide with rails 28.9%; up to 5 writes to one cell in one tick). So dropping
`from`/`to` entirely and relying on "the co-located semantic event tells you where it happened" is
wrong precisely where multiple writes collide — there is no way to tell which write the semantic
event's position refers to.

**Corrected scope of the compaction:** `tick`/`subtick` fields ARE still droppable (recoverable
from `globalSeq` plus a tick-boundary index — see `util tools/simlog_ticks.py`, added when this was
caught) and `targetKey` (old state) is still droppable (it's the replayed board — proven by
`util tools/check_log_completeness.py`'s REPLAY check, 557,363/557,363). **Position (`from`/`to`)
must stay.** So the compact record is `(globalSeq, position, newState, flags)`, not
`(globalSeq, newState, flags)` — roughly 15–20 bytes against the current 96, ~80–84% off this kind,
not the ~94% implied by the original wording. Still the single largest lever in the format; just
smaller than first stated. Not yet implemented.

### Confirmed NOT redundant, despite superficial appearance

- **PistonNeighborNotified (15)** — re-tested this session's context; residual 9-27% has no
  accompanying write. Keep.
- **PistonQueued (0)** vs **PistonMoveExecuted (1)** — `scheduledTick` on the executed record makes
  0 look absorbable, but two thirds of queues never produce a move, so 0 is the only record of the
  attempt.
- **BlockSettled (18)** — flight duration is not a constant, `SEM_*` cause distinguishes 5 different
  code paths landing at the same tick with different ordering implications.
- **RailShapeChanged (21)** — the one truly non-inferable state change; already noted above.

### New to this pass, not previously flagged

**Kinds 4 and 8 are structurally identical fan-outs of kinds 3 and 5/6 respectively** — "pulse/
appearance at position X reaches piston Y" — and in both cases the static relation is *already*
shipped separately as `WouldPowerEdge` (which also carries the quasi-connectivity distinction
neither 4 nor 8 encodes). Previously judged "keep anyway" on the grounds that removing them forces
a model to learn the fan-out rule with zero supervision — that judgement stands, but it's worth
being explicit that **this is the only reason they're kept**, not a correctness necessity. If log
size becomes a binding constraint, kinds 4/7/8 are the next-cheapest cut after the kind-22
compaction above, in that order (4 is the largest at 4.44%).

**Kinds 5 and 6 remain the closest thing to a genuinely free cut.** They are 1:1 with a
`BlockPushed`/`BlockSettled` of the same block in the same tick whenever the redstone block moves
(carried by a piston), and 1:1 with kind 22 whenever it's placed/broken directly — and
`InitialBlockState.blockTypeId` already tells a reader it's a redstone block. Populating their
position fields this session (fixing the "100% at origin" bug) makes them *more* useful, which
cuts slightly against removing them now, but if trimming volume matters they're the best
candidate: removing them loses only "a redstone block's appear/disappear is now witnessed
one hop earlier than the RedstoneActivatedPiston/DeactivatedPiston that immediately follows."

### No change from measurement

Nothing in this pass found a *new* correctness gap — the three fixes landed earlier this session
(BlockPushed payload, head/retract arm coverage, PistonQueued dedup flag) were verified via
`util tools/check_log_completeness.py` (REPLAY 100%, RESUME 100% across all 9 fixtures) and the
55-fixture behaviour-unchanged snapshot, both already passing. This pass is purely descriptive/
redundancy analysis on top of that verified state.

